>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

I went with the verify-then-dereference approach.  I wrote three
helper functions in pintos/src/userprog/syscall.c:
  valid_byte (p)      checks p != NULL, p < PHYS_BASE, and the page
                      is actually mapped (pagedir_get_page ()).
  valid_range (p, n)  accepts zero length; otherwise rejects address
                      wrap-around, checks the first and last byte,
                      and one byte on every page in between.
  valid_string (s)    walks to the NUL byte, re-checking at the start
                      and on each page boundary.  Gives up after one
                      page (4096 bytes) to avoid scanning forever.
In the handler, I first validate the stack word holding the syscall
number and the 1+N argument words (this is why sc-bad-sp and
sc-bad-arg are caught before anything gets read).  Then I copy the
arguments into a kernel-side array and dispatch.  Buffers for
read/write go through valid_range () and filename strings for
exec/create/open/remove go through valid_string (), always before
taking fs_lock.  If any check fails I call syscall_exit (-1).

As a backstop, I also modified kill () in
pintos/src/userprog/exception.c to set exit_code = -1 before calling
thread_exit (), so user-mode faults like bad-read or bad-jump still
print the "name: exit(-1)" message and clean up properly.  I added a
check in page_fault () so that a kernel-mode fault on a user address
kills the process instead of panicking the kernel.

I considered the get_user ()/put_user () approach with fault recovery
but decided against it -- it would be faster, but it needs inline
assembly helpers and makes resource cleanup from the fault path
harder to get right.  The explicit page-table checks felt simpler to
reason about and debug.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full 4,096-byte page to be read
>> from or written to user memory.  What is the least number of
>> inspections of the page table (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that
>> might result?  What is the greatest number of inspections?  What
>> about for a system call that reads or writes two consecutive bytes?
>> Is your code capable of being modified to read or write only parts of
>> a page in user memory?

Looking at my valid_range (uaddr, size) implementation:
- 4,096-byte copy:
  Least: 2 calls to pagedir_get_page ().  This happens when the
  buffer is page-aligned, so valid_byte (start) and valid_byte (last)
  land on the same page and the loop doesn't run.
  Most: 3 calls.  When the buffer straddles a page boundary, start
  is on one page and last is on the next, so I get one call for
  start, one for last, and one from the loop for the boundary page.
- Two consecutive bytes:
  Always exactly 2 calls -- valid_byte (start) and valid_byte (last).
  The loop body never executes since there's no intermediate page.
- Partial page transfer:
  Right now I validate the entire buffer upfront and kill the process
  if any part is invalid (all-or-nothing).  It could be changed to
  do page-by-page transfers, stopping at the first unmapped page,
  but I didn't need that for this project.

>> B5: How does your code ensure that wait() will fail for a given
>> process id if the process was not created by the calling process, or
>> if the calling process has already called wait() on that process?

In process_wait (), I scan cur->children looking for a child_status
whose tid matches child_tid.  If there's no match -- maybe the tid is
invalid, or it belongs to a grandchild -- I return -1 right away.
When I do find a match, I block on cs->exit_sema until the child
exits, read cs->exit_code, and then immediately remove cs from the
children list and release my reference.  Because I remove it from the
list straight away, any second call to wait () on the same tid won't
find it and will return -1.

>> B6: Any access to user memory could fail if the user passed an
>> invalid pointer.  Why does your code not leak resources when this
>> happens?  Do you have to make any special effort to prevent resource
>> leaks in such cases?

I validate every user pointer, string, and buffer before allocating
or acquiring any kernel resource (like a file descriptor, malloc
memory, or the fs_lock).  Syscall numbers and arguments are checked
before I read them, and user strings/buffers are validated before I
take fs_lock.  If any validation fails, syscall_exit (-1) triggers
process_exit (), which closes all open files in fd_list, frees child
status structures, signals exit_sema, and destroys the page directory.
Since I always validate first and cleanup is all centralized in
process_exit (), there's no path that can leak resources.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: How does your code ensure that exec() will not return until the
>> child process has successfully loaded its executable?  What
>> happens if the load fails?

In process_execute (), I allocate a child_status with load_sema = 0
and load_ok = false, then call thread_create ().  The parent
immediately blocks on sema_down (&cs->load_sema).  Over in the child
thread, start_process () calls load ().  Once load finishes (pass or
fail), the child writes the result into cs->load_ok, sets
cs->load_done = true, and does sema_up (&cs->load_sema) to wake the
parent.  The parent then checks cs->load_ok: if true, it returns the
child's tid; if false, it removes cs from the children list, drops
its reference, and returns TID_ERROR.

There's also a safety net: if the child gets killed before it even
finishes loading (e.g., it page-faults during load), process_exit ()
checks !cs->load_done and ups load_sema anyway, so the parent never
deadlocks.

>> B8: Consider parent process P and child process C.  Some possible
>> termination orders are:
>>   1. P terminates before C.
>>   2. C terminates before P calls wait() on C.
>>   3. C terminates after P calls wait() on C.
>> In each case, describe the events that occur, including the timing of
>> the release of the process's resources.

Each child_status starts with ref_cnt = 2 (one for the parent, one
for the child), protected by ref_lock.

1. P exits before C:
   When P calls process_exit (), it walks cur->children and calls
   release_status () on each one, dropping ref_cnt to 1.  P's thread
   page gets freed.  Later when C exits, it sets cs->exit_code, ups
   exit_sema (nobody's listening, but that's fine), and calls
   release_status () which drops ref_cnt to 0 and frees the struct.
   No memory leaks.

2. C exits before P waits:
   C's process_exit () stores its exit code, does sema_up on
   exit_sema, and drops its reference (ref_cnt goes to 1).  C's
   thread page and user memory get freed, but the child_status stays
   alive in P's children list.  When P eventually calls
   process_wait (), sema_down returns immediately since the semaphore
   was already upped.  P reads the exit code, removes cs from the
   list, and releases its reference, freeing the child_status.

3. P waits while C is still running:
   P finds C's entry in cur->children and blocks on exit_sema.  When
   C finishes and calls process_exit (), it records the exit code,
   ups exit_sema (waking P), and drops its reference.  P wakes up,
   grabs the exit code, removes cs, and calls release_status () to
   free it.

>> B9: The file system is not thread-safe.  How do you protect it?

I use a single global fs_lock.  load () holds it for all its file
operations, and fd_close () / fd_close_all () acquire it around
file_close ().  I always validate user memory before taking the lock,
and I never hold it while waiting for a child or creating a process,
so there's no way it can cause a deadlock.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B10: What is your rationale for choosing how to handle bad file
>> descriptors (e.g. negative values, descriptors not opened by this
>> process, stdin/stdout misuse)?

If a process passes a bad file descriptor -- negative, not open,
writing to stdin, reading from stdout -- I return an error value (-1
for read/write/filesize/tell, false for close, and just ignore seek)
instead of killing the process.  My reasoning is that a bad fd is a
normal runtime error, not a memory violation.  The process should be
able to check the return value and handle it, similar to how Unix
systems work.  A bad *pointer* is different -- that's a protection
fault and the process has to die.  This approach also lines up with
what the tests expect (read-bad-fd, write-stdin, etc. all check
that the process survives and gets -1 back).

>> B11: How does your implementation map a process id to a kernel
>> thread id?

In Pintos each user process is backed by exactly one kernel thread,
so I just use the tid as the pid directly.  pid_t and tid_t are the
same thing and I don't need any kind of translation table.

>> B12: How does your implementation prevent writes to running
>> executables?  Why is the file kept open rather than only holding
>> an inode or relying on an external lock?

After load () successfully reads the ELF segments and sets up the
stack, I call file_deny_write (file) and store the open file handle
in t->executable instead of closing it at the done: label.

The key idea is that Pintos ties write-denial to the open file handle.
file_deny_write () increments inode->deny_write_cnt, and file_close ()
automatically calls file_allow_write () to decrement it.  If I closed
the file right after loading, writes would be allowed again
immediately, which defeats the purpose.  By keeping the file open for
the entire lifetime of the process, writes stay denied until the
process exits.

For concurrent executions of the same binary (like in rox-multichild),
each process opens its own struct file pointing to the same inode.
Each one increments deny_write_cnt, so writes remain blocked until
every instance terminates and closes its handle.

In process_exit (), I close cur->executable under fs_lock *before*
signaling exit_sema.  This ordering matters because the parent needs
to see writes re-enabled by the time wait () returns (the rox-child
test checks this).

If load () fails partway through, the file gets closed at the done:
label as usual, so there's no handle leak.  And if the process dies
from an exception, thread_exit () still goes through process_exit (),
so cur->executable always gets cleaned up.

